## 2. Start the trace *before* you trigger anything

Traces are much easier to read if they start from an empty NGAP/NAS state.
Pick one:

### Option A — live view in Wireshark (recommended for a first look)
```bash
./scripts/06-wireshark-live.sh --all-kind
```
This opens Wireshark immediately, streaming from every kind node. Leave it
running, then go to §3 to trigger the test. Stop it by closing the Wireshark
window.

### Option B — capture to a file (better if you want to replay/share it)
```bash
./scripts/07-capture-to-file.sh --all-kind
```
This blocks and captures until you press `Ctrl-C`. Do that *after*
finishing §3. It prints the saved path, e.g. `cluster/capture-20260916-...pcap`.

### Narrowing the capture (optional)
Both scripts accept a `FILTER` env var (a tcpdump capture filter) if you
only care about one thing, e.g.:
```bash
FILTER='sctp or port 8805 or port 2152' ./scripts/06-wireshark-live.sh --all-kind
```
- `sctp` → N2 (NGAP, carries N1/NAS too)
- `port 8805` → N4 (PFCP)
- `port 2152` → N3/N9 (GTP-U)
- add `or tcp port 80` to also catch SBI (HTTP/2) traffic between NFs

