## 4. Watch it happen live (logs)

While the capture is running, tail the UE's own log — it narrates the whole
flow from the UE's point of view, which is the easiest single place to see
whether the attach succeeded:

```bash
POD=$(kubectl -n core get pods -l component=ue -o jsonpath='{.items[0].metadata.name}')
kubectl -n core logs -f "$POD"
```

Expected output for a successful attach (abridged):
```
[nas] [info] UE switches to state [MM-REGISTER-INITIATED]
[nas] [debug] Authentication Request received
[nas] [debug] Security Mode Command received
[nas] [debug] Registration accept received
[nas] [info] Initial Registration is successful
[nas] [debug] Sending PDU Session Establishment Request
[nas] [debug] PDU Session Establishment Accept received
[nas] [info] PDU Session establishment is successful PSI[1]
[app]  [info] Connection setup for PDU session[1] is successful, TUN interface[uesimtun0, 10.1.0.x] is up.
```
Once you see the last line, the session is up — go test data-plane traffic
(§6) or stop the capture (§2).

To see the *network* side of the same events, tail AMF and SMF in separate
terminals:
```bash
kubectl -n core logs -f deploy/core-free5gc-amf-amf
kubectl -n core logs -f deploy/core-free5gc-smf-smf
```
AMF's log shows `Handle Registration Request` → `Authentication procedure`
→ `Send Registration Accept`. SMF's shows `HandlePDUSessionEstablishmentRequest`
→ `Sending PFCP Session Establishment Request` → the SMContext being created.

