## 6. Generate actual data-plane traffic

Registration alone never produces GTP-U packets — you need to make the UE
actually send something over its new TUN interface:

```bash
POD=$(kubectl -n core get pods -l component=ue -o jsonpath='{.items[0].metadata.name}')
kubectl -n core exec -t "$POD" -- ping -I uesimtun0 -c4 8.8.8.8
```

While this runs, you should see in the trace:
- 4 GTP-U frames UE→UPF (uplink, N3) each wrapping an ICMP Echo Request
- 4 GTP-U frames UPF→UE (downlink, N3) each wrapping an ICMP Echo Reply
- plain (non-GTP) ICMP packets on the UPF's N6 interface (172.29.40.200) to/from 8.8.8.8 — this is the UPF decapsulating and forwarding onto the "internet" (the kind doc
ker bridge)

---

## 7. Quick reference: capture filters by question

| You want to see... | Filter |
|---|---|
| Everything about one UE attach | `sctp or (tcp.port==80 and http2) or pfcp or gtp` |
| Just the NAS conversation | `ngap` then Follow → NAS-5GS |
| Just the auth handshake | `http2 and (http2.header.path contains "ueau" or http2.header.path contains "ue-authentications")` |
| Just the PDU session setup | `pfcp or (ngap.procedureCode == 5)` (5 = PDUSessionResourceSetup) |
| Just user data | `gtp` |
| One specific NF's SBI calls | `tcp.port==80 and ip.addr==<pod IP, see reference doc §4>` |

---

## 8. Cross-referencing pcap timestamps with logs

`kubectl logs` timestamps and Wireshark's frame timestamps are both wall
clock, so you can correlate them directly. If you saved a `.pcap` (§2,
option B), grab the exact times of interest from the UE/AMF/SMF logs first
(they print millisecond-precision timestamps), then in Wireshark:
`Edit → Find Packet → search by "Frame time"`, or just eyeball the time
column with those log timestamps as anchors.

